---
// Mount point for lesson 8's event triage. The intro screen is server-rendered
// so the lesson still says something without JavaScript, and so the opening
// copy lives in one place. Everything after the start button is built at
// runtime by src/scripts/courses/triage/triage.ts.
//
// Nothing is held back: the lesson makes its argument before this runs, and the
// exercise is about noticing how hard the argument is to follow in front of a
// live feed. Reading ahead spoils nothing, because knowing the answer and
// remembering to check are different skills and this one drills the second.
import "@styles/triage.css";
---

<div data-triage>
  <div class="tr not-prose my-8">
    <div data-tr-stage>
      <div class="tr-shell">
        <div class="tr-intro">
          <p>
            Five feeds, five situations. Each one has a second command available
            that tells you what is actually true, and you never have to run it.
          </p>
          <div class="tr-foot">
            <button
              type="button"
              class="tr-btn"
              data-tone="primary"
              data-go="start">Open the terminal</button
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <noscript>
      <p class="tr-note mt-3">
        This exercise needs JavaScript. The short version: an event is a record
        of a moment that has passed, it expires within the hour, repeats are
        folded into one object with a counter, and none of that changes what the
        object it happened to currently says. Read the object.
      </p>
    </noscript>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import "@scripts/courses/triage/triage";
</script>
